#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055543} :activity/id #uuid "f340cd26-ce61-5815-8567-bd1eec9dd831" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055969 :tutorial/content "Recall that logarithms that share a common base $n$ obey the following three laws:\n\n- **The Product Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) + \\log_n(b) = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\n- **The Quotient Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\n- **The Power Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can use these laws together to simplify a logarithmic expression.\n\nFor instance, we can write $3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y)$ as an expression containing a single term.\n\nFirst, using the power rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) &= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2\\left(y^{1/ 2}\\right)\\\\\n\t&= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right)\t.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "435a21a1-8874-500c-93b0-c742ac5f2a73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2796 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cdc68959-41d1-54af-880a-9fe77d7686e4" :step/math-academy-id 14488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054537 :question/id #uuid "5a566673-4529-57b8-87c9-d5a6118e0398" :question/math-academy-id "e-4082" :question/problem "Express $\\log(3)+4\\log(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the second term to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log(3)+\\log(y^4).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dc94edb-007d-5fe5-b9ca-9ed754adc457" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543531}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Power Rules of Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "7a857f2e-0011-59d4-bd8a-0f70b2579888" :step/math-academy-id 14489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055545}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054623 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054538 :question/id #uuid "b0e6a6fc-18fa-57d8-9b4a-2fbd1d7fbb9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4083" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite both terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(x^3\\right) - \\log(25) =\\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5) =  \\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2c2df20-2d53-5c2e-a633-9442e118a6b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600834}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Quotient and Power Rules of Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "081ac78b-27ad-5fe5-abca-cb4412960b2f" :step/math-academy-id 14490 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054539 :question/id #uuid "c6e57845-614f-5be1-8551-d85d4ffc490e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4085" :question/problem "Express $\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule for natural logarithms, we can combine the first two terms of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\ln(2x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln \\left( \\dfrac{2x}{y} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22382eaf-2654-5c65-b7ba-ac71015e84b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050542}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608313} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608308}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Quotient Rules of Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "77abf393-81f1-588c-a957-54fc5126747f" :step/math-academy-id 14492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{3})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd9426d-8ce3-564d-85c5-4ae82289325d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608381} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608372}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product, Quotient, and Power Rules of Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "e8f6f26f-9856-546e-9046-04757e1e1bb2" :step/math-academy-id 14503}] :activity/title "Combining the Laws of Logarithms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054892}]}
